linkageBound -- computes a bound on the number of general links of an ideal to test the licci property
******************************************************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        b = linkageBound I
  * Inputs:
      * I, an *note ideal: (Macaulay2Doc)Ideal,, 
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * *note UseNormalModule: UseNormalModule, => ..., default value false,
        option for linkageBound and isLicci
  * Outputs:
      * b, an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, 

Description
===========

An ideal I in a polynomial ring S is licci if it Cohen-Macaulay and is linked
in finitely many steps I --> (F):I, where F is a maximal regular sequence in I,
to a complete intersection. Bernd Ulrich showed that if I is licci and each
step of the linkage is done via a regular sequence F that is a subset of a
minimal set of generators, then the linkage process will terminate after at
most b steps, where

b = 2(codim I)*(degree I -1) -6.

(Theorem 2.4 of "On Licci Ideals", Contemp. Math 88 (1989). This is computed by
linkageBound I. He did this via a more refined formula; the (generally sharper)
intermediate result gives the bound

b = 2(numgens(Hom(I, S/I) - codim I).

The call linkageBound(I, UseNormalModule =>true) computes this refined bound.
See isLicci for examples.

Caveat
======

The crude bound can be quite large; computing the refined bound (which is often
large as well) can be quite slow.

See also
========

  * *note isLicci: isLicci, -- Tests whether an ideal is licci
  * *note UseNormalModule: UseNormalModule, -- option for linkageBound and
    isLicci

Ways to use linkageBound:
=========================

  * "linkageBound(Ideal)"

For the programmer
==================

The object *note linkageBound: linkageBound, is a *note method function with
options: (Macaulay2Doc)MethodFunctionWithOptions,.

